<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Motupalli</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Motupalli is a coastal village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, that was a major seaport of the Kakatiya dynasty and later of the Reddi kingdom. Around 1245 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganapati-kakatiya">Ganapatideva</a> issued an inscription there guaranteeing fair treatment to foreign merchants, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> described the port, which he called Mutfili, at the end of the 13th century. It traded in textiles and diamonds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/motupalli.jpg" alt="Motupalli" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Torchtelangana · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MOTUPALLI_TEMPLE_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
